/td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>9</td>\n      <td>2008</td>\n      <td>WD</td>\n      <td>Normal</td>\n      <td>223500</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":6},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The trap you were looking for: MSSubClass\n\nIt’s stored as a number (20, 60, …), but the numbers are codes for a type of house:","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes (columns):** 79 features: 36 stored as numbers and 43 as text. MSSubClass is stored as a number but is a code for the type of dwelling (20, 60, ...), so I treat it as categorical. Several text columns are ordered quality scales (Po < Fa < TA < Gd < Ex), which could later be mapped to numbers. Columns like Alley, PoolQC and Fence are mostly missing, which in this dataset usually means the house doesn't have that feature.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Next step: missing values by column. We’ll list which columns have gaps, how many, and decide which NaNs mean “none” versus “unknown.”","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"missing = train.isna().sum()                         # number of NaNs in each column\nmissing = missing[missing > 0].sort_values(ascending=False)   # keep only columns with gaps, worst first\n\nreport = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"missing\": missing,\n    \"percent\": (100 * missing / len(train)).round(1),         # as a % of the 1,460 rows\n})\nprint(len(report), \"columns have missing values\")\nprint(report)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.675197Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.675814Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.693556Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.675787Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.692495Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"19 columns have missing values\n              missing  percent\nPoolQC           1453     99.5\nMiscFeature      1406     96.3\nAlley            1369     93.8\nFence            1179     80.8\nMasVnrType        872     59.7\nFireplaceQu       690     47.3\nLotFrontage       259     17.7\nGarageType         81      5.5\nGarageYrBlt        81      5.5\nGarageFinish       81      5.5\nGarageQual         81      5.5\nGarageCond         81      5.5\nBsmtExposure       38      2.6\nBsmtFinType2       38      2.6\nBsmtQual           37      2.5\nBsmtCond           37      2.5\nBsmtFinType1       37      2.5\nMasVnrArea          8      0.5\nElectrical          1      0.1\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":7},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(\"houses with a pool:\", (train[\"PoolArea\"] > 0).sum())        # expect 1460 - 1453 = 7\nprint(\"houses with no fireplace:\", (train[\"Fireplaces\"] == 0).sum())  # expect 690\nprint(\"houses with no garage:\", (train[\"GarageArea\"] == 0).sum())     # expect 81","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.694724Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.695024Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.720215Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.694999Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.719052Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"houses with a pool: 7\nhouses with no fireplace: 690\nhouses with no garage: 81\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":8},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"We will fill missing values by following way \n\nType 1: “It doesn’t exist” (the blank is an answer)\nHow to fill it:\n\nText columns (e.g. GarageQual) → the word “None”, which becomes its own category\nNumber columns (e.g. GarageYrBlt, MasVnrArea) → usually 0\n\nType 2: “It exists, but nobody wrote it down” (truly unknown)\n\nHow to fill it:\n\nNumbers → the median (a typical value)\nText → the most frequent value\nLearned from the training data only, inside the pipeline","metadata":{},"attachments":{"477027f7-b75d-429d-9588-3746ac299e2c.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"none_cols = [\"PoolQC\", \"MiscFeature\", \"Alley\", \"Fence\", \"FireplaceQu\", \"MasVnrType\",\n             \"GarageType\", \"GarageFinish\", \"GarageQual\", \"GarageCond\",\n             \"BsmtQual\", \"BsmtCond\", \"BsmtExposure\", \"BsmtFinType1\", \"BsmtFinType2\"]\nzero_cols = [\"GarageYrBlt\", \"MasVnrArea\"]\n\ndef prepare(df):\n    df = df.drop(columns=[\"Id\"]).copy()                  # Id is a row number, not a feature\n    df[\"MSSubClass\"] = df[\"MSSubClass\"].astype(str)      # numeric code -> category (the trap we found)\n    df[none_cols] = df[none_cols].fillna(\"None\")         # type 1: \"doesn't have it\" -> its own category\n    df[zero_cols] = df[zero_cols].fillna(0)              # type 1, numeric: no garage / no veneer -> 0\n    return df\n\nX = prepare(train.drop(columns=[\"SalePrice\"]))          # features for learning\ny = np.log1p(train[\"SalePrice\"])                         # log target (matches the competition metric)\nX_test = prepare(test)                                   # same treatment for the Kaggle test set\ntest_ids = test[\"Id\"]                                    # keep Ids for the submission file later","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.72161Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.721948Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.777106Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.721915Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.776045Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"code","source":"left = X.isna().sum()\nprint(left[left > 0])                                    # only type-2 columns should remain","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.778418Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.778707Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.792768Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.778684Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.791558Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"LotFrontage    259\nElectrical       1\ndtype: int64\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":10},{"cell_type":"code","source":"left = X.isna().sum()\nprint(left[left > 0])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.79395Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.794305Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.825104Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.794278Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.824062Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"LotFrontage    259\nElectrical       1\ndtype: int64\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":11},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Part 2: the pipeline (preprocessing + model in one object)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder\n\nnum_cols = X.select_dtypes(include=\"number\").columns.tolist()     # 35 numeric (MSSubClass is now text)\ncat_cols = X.select_dtypes(exclude=\"number\").columns.tolist()     # 44 text columns\n\npreprocess = ColumnTransformer([\n    (\"num\", make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\"),          # LotFrontage: median of the training rows\n                          StandardScaler()), num_cols),\n    (\"cat\", make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\"most_frequent\"),   # Electrical: most common value\n                          OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\")), cat_cols),\n])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.826434Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.826804Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:51.522863Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:49.826754Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:51.52186Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":12},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Numeric columns: fill the gaps with the median, then standardise.\nText columns: fill the gaps with the most common value, then one-hot encode.\nhandle_unknown=\"ignore\": if the test set contains a category that training never saw, it becomes all zeros instead of crashing.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"Xt = preprocess.fit_transform(X)                     # fit on the training data (only to look at the shape)\nprint(\"rows, columns after preprocessing:\", Xt.shape)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:51.524318Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:51.524924Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:51.625645Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:51.524885Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:51.624617Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"rows, columns after preprocessing: (1460, 316)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":13},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Next we’re going to score the baseline and the linear model with 5-fold CV.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from sklearn.model_selection import KFold, cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.dummy import DummyRegressor\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\ncv = KFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)     # same 5 folds for both models (fair comparison)\n\nmodels = {\n    \"mean baseline\": DummyRegressor(strategy=\"mean\"),\n    \"linear regression\": make_pipeline(preprocess, LinearRegression()),\n}\n\nfor name, model in models.items():\n    scores = -cross_val_score(model, X, y, cv=cv, scoring=\"neg_root_mean_squared_error\")\n    print(f\"{name:>17}:  mean {scores.mean():.4f}   per fold {scores.round(3)}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:51.630149Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:51.630523Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:53.222364Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:51.630496Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:53.221076Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"    mean baseline:  mean 0.3988   per fold [0.433 0.396 0.375 0.421 0.368]\nlinear regression:  mean 0.1528   per fold [0.137 0.125 0.232 0.155 0.115]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":14},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes (CV scores, log scale):** The mean baseline scores 0.399, close to the standard deviation of log price, as expected. Linear regression in a leakage-free pipeline scores 0.153 on average (about ±16 % of the price), a 62 % reduction. Four folds are between 0.115 and 0.155, but fold 3 scores 0.232. With 316 columns for 1,460 rows and a few known extreme houses (very large living area, low price), I suspect high-influence points or rare categories; I'll check this in the diagnostics notebook.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"* Leaky: fit_transform(X) runs once on all 1,460 rows, so each validation fold has already influenced the medians, means, stds and the list of categories. Then CV only refits the model.\n* Honest: the pipeline refits the preprocessing inside every fold, using only that fold’s training rows.\n* Everything else is identical: the same folds (cv), the same model.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# WRONG way (for the experiment only): fit the preprocessing on ALL rows, then cross-validate\nXt_all = preprocess.fit_transform(X)                     # medians, means, stds and categories seen from every row\nleaky = -cross_val_score(LinearRegression(), Xt_all, y, cv=cv,\n                         scoring=\"neg_root_mean_squared_error\")\n\n# RIGHT way (what we did before): preprocessing inside the pipeline, refitted on each training fold\nhonest = -cross_val_score(make_pipeline(preprocess, LinearRegression()), X, y, cv=cv,\n                          scoring=\"neg_root_mean_squared_error\")\n\nprint(f\"leaky : mean {leaky.mean():.4f}   per fold {leaky.round(3)}\")\nprint(f\"honest: mean {honest.mean():.4f}   per fold {honest.round(3)}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:53.223657Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:53.223999Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:55.937041Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:53.223969Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:55.935888Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"leaky : mean 0.1528   per fold [0.137 0.125 0.232 0.155 0.115]\nhonest: mean 0.1528   per fold [0.137 0.125 0.232 0.155 0.115]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":15},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes (leakage experiment):** Fitting the imputer, scaler and encoder on all 1,460 rows before cross-validation gives exactly the same CV RMSE as refitting them inside each fold (0.1528, identical per fold). For ordinary least squares this is expected: standardising only rescales the weights and leaves predictions unchanged, the median imputation of LotFrontage barely moves, and categories that appear only in a validation fold get zero weight. Leakage would matter for scale-sensitive models (Ridge, kNN) and especially for target encoding or feature selection that uses the target, so I keep all preprocessing inside the pipeline anyway.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Diagnostics (Lab 4)\n\nFold 3 scored 0.232 against 0.115–0.155 for the others. Here I look for houses that could pull the fit: unusual size and price, high leverage and high influence. Then I check residuals, multicollinearity (VIF) and a small OLS summary.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"big = train[\"GrLivArea\"] > 4000                       # houses over 4,000 sq ft (the dataset author's warning)\n\nplt.scatter(train[\"GrLivArea\"], train[\"SalePrice\"], s=8, alpha=0.4, label=\"all houses\")\nplt.scatter(train.loc[big, \"GrLivArea\"], train.loc[big, \"SalePrice\"],\n            s=40, color=\"red\", label=\"over 4,000 sq ft\")       # highlight the big ones\nplt.xlabel(\"GrLivArea (above-ground living area, sq ft)\")\nplt.ylabel(\"SalePrice\")\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()\n\nprint(train.loc[big, [\"Id\", \"GrLivArea\", \"SalePrice\", \"SaleCondition\"]])   # who are they?","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:55.938301Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:55.938749Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.172677Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:55.938689Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.171518Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"        Id  GrLivArea  SalePrice SaleCondition\n523    524       4676     184750       Partial\n691    692       4316     755000        Normal\n1182  1183       4476     745000       Abnorml\n1298  1299       5642     160000       Partial\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":16},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes (large houses):** Four houses have more than 4,000 sq ft of living area. Two of them (Id 692 and 1183, about 750k) follow the overall trend: high leverage but low influence. The other two (Id 524 and 1299) are the largest houses in the data but sold for only 185k and 160k, far below the trend, so they have both high leverage and large residuals and can tilt the fit. Both are \"Partial\" sales (sold before construction was finished), which suggests they don't represent normal market prices.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Next we’re going to find which fold each suspicious house was tested in","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"suspects = [523, 1298]                                   # row positions of Id 524 and Id 1299\n\nfor fold, (train_idx, val_idx) in enumerate(cv.split(X), start=1):   # the same 5 folds as before\n    inside = [r for r in suspects if r in val_idx]       # suspects in this fold's validation part\n    print(f\"fold {fold}: validation rows {len(val_idx)}   suspects in validation: {inside}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.174083Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.174405Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.18393Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.174379Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.182853Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"fold 1: validation rows 292   suspects in validation: []\nfold 2: validation rows 292   suspects in validation: []\nfold 3: validation rows 292   suspects in validation: [523, 1298]\nfold 4: validation rows 292   suspects in validation: []\nfold 5: validation rows 292   suspects in validation: []\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":17},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Next we’re going to replay round 3 and look at those two houses","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"for fold, (tr, va) in enumerate(cv.split(X), start=1):\n    if fold == 3:\n        break                                            # stop at round 3, keeping its train/test rows\n\nmodel3 = make_pipeline(preprocess, LinearRegression()).fit(X.iloc[tr], y.iloc[tr])   # train on folds 1,2,4,5\npred3 = model3.predict(X.iloc[va])                                                     # predict fold 3\nerr = pd.Series(pred3 - y.iloc[va].to_numpy(), index=va)                               # error per house (log scale)\n\nfor r in suspects:\n    print(f\"row {r}: actual ${train.loc[r, 'SalePrice']:,}   predicted ${np.expm1(pred3[list(va).index(r)]):,.0f}   log error {err[r]:+.2f}\")\n\nprint(f\"\\ntypical house: median |log error| {err.abs().median():.3f}\")\nprint(f\"fold 3 RMSE with the two houses:    {np.sqrt((err ** 2).mean()):.3f}\")\nprint(f\"fold 3 RMSE without the two houses: {np.sqrt((err.drop(suspects) ** 2).mean()):.3f}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.185187Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.185637Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.536326Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.18561Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.535441Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"row 523: actual $184,750   predicted $934,018   log error +1.62\nrow 1298: actual $160,000   predicted $2,820,568   log error +2.87\n\ntypical house: median |log error| 0.056\nfold 3 RMSE with the two houses:    0.232\nfold 3 RMSE without the two houses: 0.129\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":18},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes (fold 3):** Both large, cheap houses (Id 524 and 1299) fall in fold 3's validation set. Trained without them, the model predicts 934k and 2.82M for houses that sold for 185k and 160k: log errors of +1.62 and +2.87, against a median absolute error of 0.056. Without these two rows, fold 3's RMSE drops from 0.232 to 0.129, in line with the other folds. Because they are unfinished (Partial) sales of the two largest houses and the dataset's author recommends excluding houses over 4,000 sq ft, I remove them from the training data. I never drop rows from the test set.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Next we’re going to remove the two houses and rerun CV","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"keep = ~train[\"Id\"].isin([524, 1299])         # True for every house except the two unusual sales\nX_clean = X[keep]                             # features without them\ny_clean = y[keep]                             # log prices without them\nprint(\"rows:\", len(X), \"->\", len(X_clean))\n\nscores = -cross_val_score(make_pipeline(preprocess, LinearRegression()),\n                          X_clean, y_clean, cv=cv, scoring=\"neg_root_mean_squared_error\")\nprint(f\"linear regression (cleaned):  mean {scores.mean():.4f}   per fold {scores.round(3)}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.537477Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.538271Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:47:58.051367Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:56.538234Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:47:58.050458Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"rows: 1460 -> 1458\nlinear regression (cleaned):  mean 0.1345   per fold [0.141 0.144 0.127 0.152 0.109]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":19},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes (after removing two sales):** Without Id 524 and 1299 (1,458 rows), the CV RMSE on the log scale drops from 0.153 to 0.1345, and every fold is between 0.109 and 0.152, with no outlying fold. Part of the gain comes from no longer validating on those two houses, so the CV score is slightly optimistic; the Kaggle leaderboard on the untouched test set is the real check.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Next we’re going to draw residual plots for a raw-price model and a log-price model.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_predict\n\nmodel = make_pipeline(preprocess, LinearRegression())\ny_raw = train.loc[keep, \"SalePrice\"]                         # dollars (cleaned rows)\n\npred_raw = cross_val_predict(model, X_clean, y_raw, cv=cv)    # each house predicted by a model that never saw it\npred_log = cross_val_predict(model, X_clean, y_clean, cv=cv)  # same, on the log1p target\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11, 4))\naxes[0].scatter(pred_raw, y_raw - pred_raw, s=6, alpha=0.4)   # residual = actual - predicted\naxes[0].axhline(0, color=\"black\")\naxes[0].set_title(\"Raw SalePrice target\"); axes[0].set_xlabel(\"prediction ($)\"); axes[0].set_ylabel(\"residual ($)\")\n\naxes[1].scatter(pred_log, y_clean - pred_log, s=6, alpha=0.4)\naxes[1].axhline(0, color=\"black\")\naxes[1].set_title(\"log1p(SalePrice) target\"); axes[1].set_xlabel(\"prediction (log)\"); axes[1].set_ylabel(\"residual (log)\")\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:47:58.052705Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:47:58.053053Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:48:01.348861Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:47:58.053025Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:48:01.347809Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_