","text":"GrLivArea       7.5\nGarageCars      6.2\nGarageArea      5.3\nTotRmsAbvGrd    4.8\n1stFlrSF        4.5\nYearBuilt       4.1\nTotalBsmtSF     3.7\nOverallQual     3.2\ndtype: float64\n\nCV RMSE: 0.1338   (before: 0.1346)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":24},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes (VIF, actions taken):** Two exact identities made eight area columns perfectly collinear (VIF infinite). Dropping only the mostly-zero parts (LowQualFinSF, BsmtFinSF2) removed the exact identity but left a near-exact one: GrLivArea still had VIF 121, because LowQualFinSF is zero for 98 % of houses. Also dropping 2ndFlrSF and BsmtUnfSF brings every VIF to 7.5 or below (GrLivArea 7.5, GarageCars 6.2, GarageArea 5.3). CV RMSE stayed essentially the same throughout (0.1345, 0.1346, 0.1338), which shows multicollinearity affects the interpretation of the weights, not the predictions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"features = [\"OverallQual\", \"GrLivArea\", \"TotalBsmtSF\", \"GarageCars\", \"YearBuilt\",\n            \"YearRemodAdd\", \"Fireplaces\", \"FullBath\", \"LotArea\", \"OverallCond\"]   # 10 interpretable features\n\nXs = sm.add_constant(X_clean3[features])        # raw units (not scaled), plus the column of 1s\nols = sm.OLS(y_clean, Xs).fit()                  # ordinary least squares on log1p(SalePrice)\n\ntable = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"coef\": ols.params,\n    \"% per unit\": (100 * (np.exp(ols.params) - 1)).round(2),   # log target -> % change in price per +1 unit\n    \"std err\": ols.bse,\n    \"p-value\": ols.pvalues.round(4),\n}).drop(index=\"const\")\nprint(table)\nprint(f\"\\nR² = {ols.rsquared:.3f}   adjusted R² = {ols.rsquared_adj:.3f}   condition number = {ols.condition_number:,.0f}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:48:09.384924Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:48:09.385296Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:48:09.41081Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:48:09.38526Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:48:09.408902Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"                  coef  % per unit       std err  p-value\nOverallQual   0.072899        7.56  4.327578e-03   0.0000\nGrLivArea     0.000279        0.03  1.138354e-05   0.0000\nTotalBsmtSF   0.000178        0.02  1.068740e-05   0.0000\nGarageCars    0.064396        6.65  6.484197e-03   0.0000\nYearBuilt     0.003494        0.35  1.998129e-04   0.0000\nYearRemodAdd  0.001112        0.11  2.458501e-04   0.0000\nFireplaces    0.050820        5.21  6.498890e-03   0.0000\nFullBath     -0.022722       -2.25  9.524208e-03   0.0172\nLotArea       0.000003        0.00  3.817685e-07   0.0000\nOverallCond   0.057004        5.87  3.801151e-03   0.0000\n\nR² = 0.888   adjusted R² = 0.887   condition number = 1,895,110\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":25},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Note : \n* Why p-values are a poor tool for choosing features for prediction\n* High VIF inflates p-values. Two correlated useful features can both look “not significant” (each one’s credit is uncertain), so you’d wrongly drop both.\n* “Not significant” ≠ “useless for prediction.” A small but real effect can still improve accuracy.\n* They rely on assumptions (independence, equal variance, normal errors) and are computed in-sample.\n* Small n vs large n: with lots of data almost everything becomes “significant,” even tiny effects.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Final model and submission (Lab 5)\n\nFinal pipeline: the \"doesn't have it\" fills from prepare(), median / most-frequent imputation, standardisation and one-hot encoding inside a ColumnTransformer, and LinearRegression on log1p(SalePrice). Trained on all training rows except the two unfinished sales of very large houses (Id 524, 1299), without the four area columns that were exact or near-exact sums of others. Best CV RMSE (log scale): 0.1338.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Next we’