6-11-06T13:48:11.450263Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:48:12.819668Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"CV RMSE, sizes logged only: 0.1324   per fold [0.148 0.137 0.129 0.139 0.109]\ncompare: no logging 0.1338   all 15 logged 0.1398\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":33},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes (log-transformed features):** Logging all 15 numeric columns with skew above 0.75 made CV worse (0.1398 vs 0.1338): most of them are mostly-zero extras (pools, porches, MiscVal), where the log doesn't help and the few non-zero houses get unstable weights. Logging only the six long-tailed size columns (LotArea, GrLivArea, 1stFlrSF, LotFrontage, BsmtFinSF1, MasVnrArea) gives 0.1324, the best CV so far. The gain is small compared with fold-to-fold variation, but it should also make very large test houses look less extreme to the model.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"final_v3 = make_pipeline(preprocess3, LinearRegression())\nfinal_v3.fit(X_log2, y_clean)                                    # all cleaned training rows, size columns logged\n\nX_test_v3 = log_sizes(X_test3)                                   # test gets EXACTLY the same transform\npred_v3 = np.expm1(final_v3.predict(X_test_v3))                  # back to dollars\nprint(f\"before clipping: min ${pred_v3.min():,.0f}   max ${pred_v3.max():,.0f}\")\n\npred_v3 = np.clip(pred_v3, lo, hi)                               # same clipping as version 2\nsubmission = pd.DataFrame({\"Id\": test_ids, \"SalePrice\": pred_v3})\nsubmission.to_csv(\"submission.csv\", index=False)\nprint(\"rows written:\", len(submission))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:48:12.821815Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:48:12.822071Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:48:13.181638Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:48:12.822048Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:48:13.180681Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"before clipping: min $37,087   max $841,196\nrows written: 1459\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":34},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The idea in one example\n\nNeighborhood has 25 categories. Some, like “Blueste,” appear in only 2 houses. With so few examples, the model’s weight for “Blueste” is basically a guess based on 2 sales, and it can be wild.\n\nThe fix: any category seen fewer than 10 times gets merged into one shared “infrequent” bucket. The model then learns one steadier weight for “rare stuff” instead of many wild ones. That’s fewer columns and less variance.\n\nNext we’re going to rebuild the encoder with rare-category grouping and check CV.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"preprocess4 = ColumnTransformer([\n    (\"num\", make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\"), StandardScaler()), num_cols3),\n    (\"cat\", make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\"most_frequent\"),\n                          OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"infrequent_if_exist\",   # unseen test categories -> the rare bucket\n                                        min_frequency=10)),                     # categories seen < 10 times -> merged\n     cat_cols),\n])\n\nprint(\"columns before:\", preprocess3.fit_transform(X_log2).shape[1],\n      \"  after:\", preprocess4.fit_transform(X_log2).shape[1])\n\nscores6 = -cross_val_score(make_pipeline(preprocess4, LinearRegression()),\n                           X_log2, y_clean, cv=cv, scoring=\"neg_root_mean_squared_error\")\nprint(f\"CV RMSE, rare categories grouped: {scores6.mean():.4f}   per fold {scores6.round(3)}   (before: 0.1324)\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:48:13.189444Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:48:13.189777Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:48:14.574559Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:48:13.189751Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:48:14.573506Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"columns before: 311   after: 269\nCV RMSE, rare categories grouped: 0.1228   per fold [0.13  0.12  0.122 0.13  0.111]   (before: 0.1324)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":35},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes (rare categories):** One-hot encoding gave 311 columns, many for categories seen in only a few houses, each with its own unconstrained weight. Merging categories seen fewer than 10 times into a shared \"infrequent\" column (OneHotEncoder with min_frequency=10, handle_unknown=\"infrequent_if_exist\") cuts the columns to 269 and improves CV RMSE from 0.1324 to 0.1228. Every fold improves and they are closer together (0.111–0.130), which points to lower variance rather than a lucky split.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"final_v4 = make_pipeline(preprocess4, LinearRegression())        # rare categories grouped\nfinal_v4.fit(X_log2, y_clean)                                      # all cleaned training rows, sizes logged\n\npred_v4 = np.expm1(final_v4.predict(log_sizes(X_test3)))          # same transforms on test, back to dollars\nprint(f\"before clipping: min ${pred_v4.min():,.0f}   max ${pred_v4.max():,.0f}\")\n\npred_v4 = np.clip(pred_v4, lo, hi)                                 # same clipping as before\npd.DataFrame({\"Id\": test_ids, \"SalePrice\": pred_v4}).to_csv(\"submission.csv\", index=False)\nprint(\"saved\", len(pred_v4), \"predictions\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T13:51:11.311057Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T13:51:11.311444Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T13:51:11.611233Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T13:51:11.311416Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T13:51:11.610219Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"before clipping: min $39,862   max $1,159,757\nsaved 1459 predictions\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":36},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes (version 4):** Grouping rare categories improves the public score from 0.14556 to 0.13492, below the 0.14 target. Summary of submissions: V1 0.15173 (pipeline, log target, two outliers removed, collinear area columns dropped), V2 0.14727 (predictions clipped to the training price range), V3 0.14556 (log of six long-tailed size columns), V4 0.13492 (categories seen fewer than 10 times merged). The public score is still above CV (0.1228); part of that gap is the very large test house whose prediction has to be clipped.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}